          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Brezhnev Doctrine</strong><br>\nThe Brezhnev Doctrine was a policy that declared the Soviet Union's right to intervene in the affairs of Communist countries to strengthen Communism. This was used to justify the invasion of Czechoslovakia in 1968.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy and Death</strong><br>\nBrezhnev's legacy is complex; he maintained Soviet stability yet is also criticized for the era of stagnation. He died in 1982, leaving a mixed legacy of stability and stagnation.</p>\n\n<p><strong>After Brezhnev</strong><br>\nFollowing his death, Brezhnev was succeeded by Yuri Andropov, the beginning of a succession of leaders that would lead to the eventual reform and dissolution of the Soviet Union under Mikhail Gorbachev.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
